## Unstated is unconstrained
The spec need not be complete. An invariant states its own boundary (which inputs it covers) and
what holds there; on an input no invariant covers, any output is acceptable. A gap is never a
finding: not "this case is not covered", "no invariant decides X" or "the predicate does not say
what happens when …", nor a value a doc or invariant leaves free. A boundary that two careful
readers would apply differently, or that a program cannot check, IS a finding.

## How to answer
Before you answer `concrete`, `decidable` and `one_owner` for an invariant, probe it: for each
clause of its predicate, try inputs at the edge of the boundary that clause draws (each way of
writing what it names, one nested inside another, one repeated or combined, one that another
invariant of the index also covers) and check that it says one thing on each. Answer `ok` only
when every probe inside the boundary has one reading.
Walk the files in order and answer every item for each; do not stop at the first failing item.
An item fails only for a concrete reason you can quote: the words at fault and, for every item
but `mentions_complete` and `no_missing_field`, a concrete input INSIDE the invariant's stated
boundary on which two careful readers would decide differently what it requires, or on which it
and another invariant require different things, or a boundary that cannot be applied alike. Do
not fail an item to ask for a rewording of a text that already says one thing, for a case
outside the boundary, nor for what an entity's doc already settles when the file reads it as the
doc defines it.
